  "authoritative_metadata": "manifest.json",
  "title": "Which handles cost: tight cuts are free, junctions are paid for by atoms",
  "status": "Proved",
  "claim": "Which handles cost. For a cubic triple system H with Pfaffian incidence graph B, any signing and any complex coupling lam (weight lam per monochromatic line; lam = 0 counts 2-colourings): (Theorem 1) the count is computed with polynomial work plus at most 8*Sum 2^c(piece) planar Pfaffians per evaluation of each Robertson-Seymour-Thomas trisum brace, c(piece) the junctions hosted by the piece whose tensor violates the matchgate identity; three-edge tight cuts, hence Fano atoms, cost nothing, and the time is poly(n) 2^J (J the number of junctions); at lam = 0 only junctions at or above squares with three twisted branches violate it (poly(n) 2^b). (Theorem 2) If B is simple and not the Heawood graph and sigma is a Pfaffian signing, J + p <= a + |sigma| - 2 (p planar braces, a Heawood braces); the bound is attained by the trisum of three cubes with three edge atoms (a 29-point plain three-fold space). (Corollaries) On every plain three-fold space every such count takes time poly(n) 2^(a-2), and is polynomial with planar glue at any number of atoms, so the #P-hard coupling families on the knot's spaces need unboundedly many atoms; off the knot's plain spaces counting is #P-hard with no atom (PLAIN-BRACE, SOFT-CROSS), so it is not FPT in the number of atoms there. Fixed-parameter tractability in the genus of B is classical (4^g determinants: Galluccio-Loebl, Tesler, Cimasoni) and is not claimed.",
